# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Swapping two variables", question.
id: swap-no-temp
title: Swapping without a temporary variable
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Swapping two variables", question
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, counterexample

task:
  Suppose that we remove the temporary variable and write the swap as
  `x := y; y := x`. Compute the weakest precondition for the same
  postcondition. For which values of $A$ and $B$ does this shorter program
  still work?

pre: x = A && y = B
post: x = B && y = A

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := y;      // i1
  {R1}
  y := x       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: The last statement, `y := x`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
calc:
  wp(y := x, Q)
  = (x = B && y = A)[y <- x]
  = x = B && x = A
set: R1 = x = B && x = A
text:
  After `y := x`, the variable $y$ holds the value of $x$. So for
  $y = A$ to hold afterwards, $x = A$ must hold before.

step: The first statement, `x := y`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(x := y, R1)
  = (x = B && x = A)[x <- y]
  = y = B && y = A
set: R2 = y = B && y = A
text:
  This is the weakest precondition of the program. It says that $y$ is
  equal to both $A$ and $B$, which is only possible when $A = B$.

step: Compare with the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = A && y = B ==> y = B && y = A
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1, y = 2, A = 1, B = 2
why:
  The precondition gives $y = B$, but nothing gives $y = A$ unless
  $A = B$. With $A = 1$ and $B = 2$ the implication is false.
text:
  Running the program from $x = 1$, $y = 2$: the first assignment gives
  $x = 2$, and the second gives $y = 2$. The original value $1$ of $x$ is
  lost, so $y = A$ is false at the end.
vc: {x = A && y = B} x := y; y := x {x = B && y = A}
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1, y = 2, A = 1, B = 2
result: The triple is not valid. The program only works when $A = B$, that is, when the two values are already equal.
